o 2 decimals)","options_en":["49","56.83","49.71","48.71"],"correct":3,"exp":"New average = (Old sum + New number)/(n+1) = (47×6 + 59)/7 = 48.71.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Averages","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_52","q_en":"The average of 5 numbers is 32. A new number 72 is added to the set. What is the new average? (round to 2 decimals)","options_en":["46.4","38.67","39.67","40"],"correct":1,"exp":"New average = (Old sum + New number)/(n+1) = (32×5 + 72)/6 = 38.67.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Averages","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_53","q_en":"The average of 5 numbers is 39. A new number 62 is added to the set. What is the new average? (round to 2 decimals)","options_en":["51.4","42.83","43.6","43.83"],"correct":1,"exp":"New average = (Old sum + New number)/(n+1) = (39×5 + 62)/6 = 42.83.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Averages","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_54","q_en":"₹385 is divided between A and B in the ratio 2:5. What is A''s share?","options_en":["192.5","275","110","132"],"correct":2,"exp":"A''s share = Total × 2/(2+5) = 385 × 2/7 = ₹110.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Ratio & Proportion","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_55","q_en":"₹216 is divided between A and B in the ratio 5:7. What is A''s share?","options_en":["90","107.63","126","108"],"correct":0,"exp":"A''s share = Total × 5/(5+7) = 216 × 5/12 = ₹90.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Ratio & Proportion","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_56","q_en":"₹50 is divided between A and B in the ratio 2:3. What is A''s share?","options_en":["30","20","24","25"],"correct":1,"exp":"A''s share = Total × 2/(2+3) = 50 × 2/5 = ₹20.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Ratio & Proportion","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_57","q_en":"₹220 is divided between A and B in the ratio 2:3. What is A''s share?","options_en":["88","110","105.6","132"],"correct":0,"exp":"A''s share = Total × 2/(2+3) = 220 × 2/5 = ₹88.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Ratio & Proportion","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_58","q_en":"₹224 is divided between A and B in the ratio 3:4. What is A''s share?","options_en":["115.2","128","112","96"],"correct":3,"exp":"A''s share = Total × 3/(3+4) = 224 × 3/7 = ₹96.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Ratio & Proportion","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_59","q_en":"₹180 is divided between A and B in the ratio 4:5. What is A''s share?","options_en":["80","100","96","90"],"correct":0,"exp":"A''s share = Total × 4/(4+5) = 180 × 4/9 = ₹80.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Ratio & Proportion","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_60","q_en":"A can complete a piece of work in 24 days and B can complete it in 40 days. Working together, in how many days will they complete the work? (round to 2 decimals)","options_en":["16","32","60","15"],"correct":3,"exp":"Combined rate = 1/24 + 1/40 per day. Time = 1/(1/24+1/40) = 15.00 days.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Time & Work","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_61","q_en":"A can complete a piece of work in 30 days and B can complete it in 10 days. Working together, in how many days will they complete the work? (round to 2 decimals)","options_en":["15","7.5","8.5","20"],"correct":1,"exp":"Combined rate = 1/30 + 1/10 per day. Time = 1/(1/30+1/10) = 7.50 days.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Time & Work","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_62","q_en":"A can complete a piece of work in 10 days and B can complete it in 40 days. Working together, in how many days will they complete the work? (round to 2 decimals)","options_en":["8","13.33","9","25"],"correct":0,"exp":"Combined rate = 1/10 + 1/40 per day. Time = 1/(1/10+1/40) = 8.00 days.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Time & Work","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_63","q_en":"A can complete a piece of work in 24 days and B can complete it in 12 days. Working together, in how many days will they complete the work? (round to 2 decimals)","options_en":["9","18","8","24"],"correct":2,"exp":"Combined rate = 1/24 + 1/12 per day. Time = 1/(1/24+1/12) = 8.00 days.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Time & Work","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_64","q_en":"A can complete a piece of work in 24 days and B can complete it in 20 days. Working together, in how many days will they complete the work? (round to 2 decimals)","options_en":["22","11.91","10.91","120"],"correct":2,"exp":"Combined rate = 1/24 + 1/20 per day. Time = 1/(1/24+1/20) = 10.91 days.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Time & Work","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_65","q_en":"A can complete a piece of work in 30 days and B can complete it in 20 days. Working together, in how many days will they complete the work? (round to 2 decimals)","options_en":["25","13","12","60"],"correct":2,"exp":"Combined rate = 1/30 + 1/20 per day. Time = 1/(1/30+1/20) = 12.00 days.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Time & Work","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_66","q_en":"A train travels at a speed of 50 km/h for 2 hours. What distance does it cover?","options_en":["110","25","100","52"],"correct":2,"exp":"Distance = Speed × Time = 50 × 2 = 100.00 km.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Time, Speed & Distance","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_67","q_en":"A train travels at a speed of 72 km/h for 3 hours. What distance does it cover?","options_en":["24","75","237.6","216"],"correct":3,"exp":"Distance = Speed × Time = 72 × 3 = 216.00 km.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Time, Speed & Distance","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_68","q_en":"A train travels at a speed of 80 km/h for 2 hours. What distance does it cover?","options_en":["82","176","40","160"],"correct":3,"exp":"Distance = Speed × Time = 80 × 2 = 160.00 km.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Time, Speed & Distance","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_69","q_en":"A train travels at a speed of 60 km/h for 2 hours. What distance does it cover?","options_en":["132","62","30","120"],"correct":3,"exp":"Distance = Speed × Time = 60 × 2 = 120.00 km.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Time, Speed & Distance","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_70","q_en":"A train travels at a speed of 90 km/h for 1.5 hours. What distance does it cover?","options_en":["60","148.5","135","91.5"],"correct":2,"exp":"Distance = Speed × Time = 90 × 1.5 = 135.00 km.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Time, Speed & Distance","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_71","q_en":"A train travels at a speed of 80 km/h for 1.5 hours. What distance does it cover?","options_en":["120","53.33","132","81.5"],"correct":0,"exp":"Distance = Speed × Time = 80 × 1.5 = 120.00 km.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Time, Speed & Distance","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_72","q_en":"Two trains of length 200 m and 120 m are moving in opposite directions at 40 km/h and 30 km/h respectively. In how many seconds will they cross each other? (round to 2 decimals)","options_en":["32.54","16.46","8.23","32.91"],"correct":1,"exp":"Relative speed = (40+30) km/h = 70×5/18 = 19.44 m/s. Total distance to cover = sum of lengths = 320 m. Time = Distance/Speed = 320/19.44 = 16.46 s.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Time, Speed & Distance","contentType":"static","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_73","q_en":"Two trains of length 120 m and 150 m are moving in opposite directions at 50 km/h and 50 km/h respectively. In how many seconds will they cross each other? (round to 2 decimals)","options_en":["19.07","9.72","4.86","19.44"],"correct":1,"exp":"Relative speed = (50+50) km/h = 100×5/18 = 27.78 m/s. Total distance to cover = sum of lengths = 270 m. Time = Distance/Speed = 270/27.78 = 9.72 s.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Time, Speed & Distance","contentType":"static","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_74","q_en":"Two trains of length 100 m and 180 m are moving in opposite directions at 40 km/h and 40 km/h respectively. In how many seconds will they cross each other? (round to 2 decimals)","options_en":["24.83","6.3","12.6","25.2"],"correct":2,"exp":"Relative speed = (40+40) km/h = 80×5/18 = 22.22 m/s. Total distance to cover = sum of lengths = 280 m. Time = Distance/Speed = 280/22.22 = 12.60 s.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Time, Speed & Distance","contentType":"static","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_75","q_en":"Two trains of length 150 m and 150 m are moving in opposite directions at 40 km/h and 30 km/h respectively. In how many seconds will they cross each other? (round to 2 decimals)","options_en":["30.86","7.71","30.49","15.43"],"correct":3,"exp":"Relative speed = (40+30) km/h = 70×5/18 = 19.44 m/s. Total distance to cover = sum of lengths = 300 m. Time = Distance/Speed = 300/19.44 = 15.43 s.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Time, Speed & Distance","contentType":"static","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_76","q_en":"What comes next in the series: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, ?","options_en":["12","11","13","15"],"correct":0,"exp":"The pattern is: add 2 each time (even numbers). So the next term is 12.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Number Series","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_77","q_en":"What comes next in the series: 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, ?","options_en":["22","17","19","18"],"correct":3,"exp":"The pattern is: add 3 each time (multiples of 3). So the next term is 18.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Number Series","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_78","q_en":"What comes next in the series: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, ?","options_en":["36","37","35","48"],"correct":0,"exp":"The pattern is: perfect squares: 1², 2², 3², 4², 5², 6². So the next term is 36.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Number Series","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_79","q_en":"What comes next in the series: 2, 6, 18, 54, 162, ?","options_en":["485","487","811","486"],"correct":3,"exp":"The pattern is: multiply by 3 each time. So the next term is 486.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Number Series","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_80","q_en":"What comes next in the series: 5, 10, 20, 40, 80, ?","options_en":["160","241","159","161"],"correct":0,"exp":"The pattern is: multiply by 2 each time. So the next term is 160.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Number Series","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_81","q_en":"What comes next in the series: 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, ?","options_en":["7","9","12","8"],"correct":3,"exp":"The pattern is: Fibonacci: each term is the sum of the two preceding terms. So the next term is 8.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Number Series","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_82","q_en":"What comes next in the series: 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, ?","options_en":["21","22","28","20"],"correct":0,"exp":"The pattern is: triangular numbers: add 2, then 3, then 4, then 5.... So the next term is 21.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Number Series","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_83","q_en":"What comes next in the series: 100, 90, 81, 73, 66, ?","options_en":["61","60","59","55"],"correct":1,"exp":"The pattern is: subtract 10, then 9, then 8, then 7, then 6 (decreasing by 1 each time). So the next term is 60.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Number Series","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_84","q_en":"Simplify: 12 + 8 × 3 − 6 ÷ 2 = ?","options_en":["35","33","31","36.3"],"correct":1,"exp":"Applying BODMAS (brackets, orders, division/multiplication, addition/subtraction in order): 12 + 8 × 3 − 6 ÷ 2 = 33.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Simplification (BODMAS)","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_85","q_en":"Simplify: (15 − 5) × 2 + 10 = ?","options_en":["33","32","30","28"],"correct":2,"exp":"Applying BODMAS (brackets, orders, division/multiplication, addition/subtraction in order): (15 − 5) × 2 + 10 = 30.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Simplification (BODMAS)","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_86","q_en":"Simplify: 36 ÷ 6 × 3 + 4 = ?","options_en":["24","24.2","20","22"],"correct":3,"exp":"Applying BODMAS (brackets, orders, division/multiplication, addition/subtraction in order): 36 ÷ 6 × 3 + 4 = 22.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Simplification (BODMAS)","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_87","q_en":"Simplify: 7 × (6 + 4) − 20 = ?","options_en":["55","50","52","48"],"correct":1,"exp":"Applying BODMAS (brackets, orders, division/multiplication, addition/subtraction in order): 7 × (6 + 4) − 20 = 50.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Simplification (BODMAS)","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_88","q_en":"Simplify: 100 − 4 × (5 + 3) = ?","options_en":["68","70","74.8","66"],"correct":0,"exp":"Applying BODMAS (brackets, orders, division/multiplication, addition/subtraction in order): 100 − 4 × (5 + 3) = 68.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Simplification (BODMAS)","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_89","q_en":"Simplify: (18 + 12) ÷ 5 × 2 = ?","options_en":["13.2","14","10","12"],"correct":3,"exp":"Applying BODMAS (brackets, orders, division/multiplication, addition/subtraction in order): (18 + 12) ÷ 5 × 2 = 12.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Simplification (BODMAS)","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_90","q_en":"Simplify: 9² − 4 × 5 = ?","options_en":["61","63","67.1","59"],"correct":0,"exp":"Applying BODMAS (brackets, orders, division/multiplication, addition/subtraction in order): 9² − 4 × 5 = 61.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Simplification (BODMAS)","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_quant_91","q_en":"Simplify: 3 × 3² + 2 × 4 = ?","options_en":["35","38.5","37","33"],"correct":0,"exp":"Applying BODMAS (brackets, orders, division/multiplication, addition/subtraction in order): 3 × 3² + 2 × 4 = 35.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Simplification (BODMAS)","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"}]'::jsonb,
  0, false, 'CSAT'
);

INSERT INTO tests_catalog (id, title, category, extra_categories, active, delisted, bundle_only, price, scheme, question_count, questions, order_num, coming_soon, topics_covered)
VALUES (
  'paper_csat_reasoning_di_1',
  'CSAT — Logical Reasoning & Data Interpretation',
  'upsc_cse',
  '["kpsc_kas"]'::jsonb,
  true, false, false, 0,
  '{"cutoff":66,"duration":120,"examBadge":"UPSC CSE","marksWrong":0.83,"marksCorrect":2.5}'::jsonb,
  47,
  '[{"id":"q_csat_reason_1","q_en":"In a certain code, each letter of a word is replaced by the letter 2 place(s) ahead of it in the alphabet (e.g., A → C). How is ''CHAIR'' written in that code?","options_en":["DIBJS","EJCKT","FKDLU","TKCJE"],"correct":1,"exp":"Shift every letter of ''CHAIR'' forward by 2: C→E, H→J, A→C, I→K, R→T. Result: EJCKT.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Coding-Decoding","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_2","q_en":"In a certain code, each letter of a word is replaced by the letter 3 place(s) ahead of it in the alphabet (e.g., A → D). How is ''TABLE'' written in that code?","options_en":["HOEDW","WDEOH","VCDNG","XEFPI"],"correct":1,"exp":"Shift every letter of ''TABLE'' forward by 3: T→W, A→D, B→E, L→O, E→H. Result: WDEOH.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Coding-Decoding","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_3","q_en":"In a certain code, each letter of a word is replaced by the letter 1 place(s) ahead of it in the alphabet (e.g., A → B). How is ''MOUSE'' written in that code?","options_en":["OQWUG","PRXVH","FTVPN","NPVTF"],"correct":3,"exp":"Shift every letter of ''MOUSE'' forward by 1: M→N, O→P, U→V, S→T, E→F. Result: NPVTF.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Coding-Decoding","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_4","q_en":"In a certain code, each letter of a word is replaced by the letter 4 place(s) ahead of it in the alphabet (e.g., A → E). How is ''PAPER'' written in that code?","options_en":["VITET","TETIV","SDSHU","UFUJW"],"correct":1,"exp":"Shift every letter of ''PAPER'' forward by 4: P→T, A→E, P→T, E→I, R→V. Result: TETIV.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Coding-Decoding","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_5","q_en":"In a certain code, each letter of a word is replaced by the letter 2 place(s) ahead of it in the alphabet (e.g., A → C). How is ''WATER'' written in that code?","options_en":["XBUFS","YCVGT","TGVCY","ZDWHU"],"correct":1,"exp":"Shift every letter of ''WATER'' forward by 2: W→Y, A→C, T→V, E→G, R→T. Result: YCVGT.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Coding-Decoding","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_6","q_en":"In a certain code, each letter of a word is replaced by the letter 3 place(s) ahead of it in the alphabet (e.g., A → D). How is ''LIGHT'' written in that code?","options_en":["OLJKW","PMKLX","WKJLO","NKIJV"],"correct":0,"exp":"Shift every letter of ''LIGHT'' forward by 3: L→O, I→L, G→J, H→K, T→W. Result: OLJKW.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Coding-Decoding","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_7","q_en":"In a code, each letter is replaced by the letter 3 place(s) BEHIND it in the alphabet (e.g., D → A). How is ''DELHI'' written in that code?","options_en":["ZAHDE","ABIEF","GHOKL","FEIBA"],"correct":1,"exp":"Shift every letter of ''DELHI'' backward by 3: D→A, E→B, L→I, H→E, I→F. Result: ABIEF.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Coding-Decoding","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_8","q_en":"If each letter of the alphabet is given its position number (A=1, B=2, ... Z=26), what is the number-code for the word ''BOAT''?","options_en":["20-1-15-2","2-15-1-20","1-14-3-19","3-16-2-21"],"correct":1,"exp":"Position numbers: B=2, O=15, A=1, T=20. So ''BOAT'' = 2-15-1-20.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Coding-Decoding","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_9","q_en":"If each letter of the alphabet is given its position number (A=1, B=2, ... Z=26), what is the number-code for the word ''RIVER''?","options_en":["18-9-22-5-18","19-10-23-6-19","17-8-21-4-17","18-5-22-9-18"],"correct":0,"exp":"Position numbers: R=18, I=9, V=22, E=5, R=18. So ''RIVER'' = 18-9-22-5-18.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Coding-Decoding","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_10","q_en":"If each letter of the alphabet is given its position number (A=1, B=2, ... Z=26), what is the number-code for the word ''CLOCK''?","options_en":["3-12-15-3-11","4-13-16-4-12","2-11-14-2-10","11-3-15-12-3"],"correct":0,"exp":"Position numbers: C=3, L=12, O=15, C=3, K=11. So ''CLOCK'' = 3-12-15-3-11.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Coding-Decoding","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_11","q_en":"Find the next term in the series: A, C, E, G, I, ?","options_en":["J","K","L","I"],"correct":1,"exp":"Letters at odd positions (1,3,5,7,9,11): skip one letter each time → next is K.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Letter Series","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_12","q_en":"Find the next term in the series: Z, Y, X, W, V, ?","options_en":["S","V","T","U"],"correct":3,"exp":"Letters in reverse order, moving back by one each time → next is U.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Letter Series","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_13","q_en":"Find the next term in the series: B, D, F, H, J, ?","options_en":["J","L","M","K"],"correct":1,"exp":"Even-position letters (2,4,6,8,10,12): skip one letter each time → next is L.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Letter Series","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_14","q_en":"Find the next term in the series: A, D, G, J, M, ?","options_en":["Q","O","N","P"],"correct":3,"exp":"Each letter is 3 places after the previous one (gap of 3) → next is P.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Letter Series","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_15","q_en":"Find the next term in the series: Y, W, U, S, Q, ?","options_en":["O","P","M","N"],"correct":0,"exp":"Each letter is 2 places before the previous one → next is O.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Letter Series","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_16","q_en":"A man starts from a point and walks 10 m North, 10 m East, 5 m South. How far is he now from the starting point? (round to 2 decimals)","options_en":["15 m","11.18 m","9.18 m","13.18 m"],"correct":1,"exp":"Taking the start as origin (0,0) with North as +y and East as +x: after the moves, his net position is (10, 5). Straight-line distance from start = √(10² + 5²) = 11.18 m.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Direction Sense","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_17","q_en":"A man starts from a point and walks 8 m East, 6 m North, 8 m West. How far is he now from the starting point? (round to 2 decimals)","options_en":["6.0 m","6 m","8.0 m","4.0 m"],"correct":0,"exp":"Taking the start as origin (0,0) with North as +y and East as +x: after the moves, his net position is (0, 6). Straight-line distance from start = √(0² + 6²) = 6.00 m.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Direction Sense","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_18","q_en":"A man starts from a point and walks 12 m South, 5 m East, 12 m North. How far is he now from the starting point? (round to 2 decimals)","options_en":["5.0 m","7.0 m","3.0 m","5 m"],"correct":0,"exp":"Taking the start as origin (0,0) with North as +y and East as +x: after the moves, his net position is (5, 0). Straight-line distance from start = √(5² + 0²) = 5.00 m.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Direction Sense","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_19","q_en":"Pointing to a photograph, Ravi said, ''She is the daughter of my mother''s only son.'' How is the girl in the photograph related to Ravi?","options_en":["Daughter","Sister","Niece","Cousin"],"correct":0,"exp":"Ravi''s mother''s only son = Ravi himself (since he is the only son). So the girl is Ravi''s own daughter.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Blood Relations","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_20","q_en":"A is the brother of B. B is the sister of C. C is the father of D. How is A related to D?","options_en":["Uncle","Father","Grandfather","Brother"],"correct":0,"exp":"A is B''s brother, so A and B are siblings. C is D''s father, and B is C''s sister, so B is D''s aunt — making A, B''s brother, D''s uncle.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Blood Relations","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_21","q_en":"Pointing to a man, a woman said, ''His mother is the only daughter of my mother.'' How is the woman related to the man?","options_en":["Mother","Sister","Aunt","Grandmother"],"correct":0,"exp":"The only daughter of the woman''s mother = the woman herself (since she is the only daughter). So the man''s mother is the woman herself — the woman is the man''s mother.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Blood Relations","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_22","q_en":"P is Q''s father. Q has one sibling, R. S is R''s mother. How is S related to P?","options_en":["Wife","Sister","Daughter","Mother"],"correct":0,"exp":"S is the mother of R, and R is Q''s sibling, so S is also Q''s mother. Since P is Q''s father and S is Q''s mother, S is P''s wife.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Blood Relations","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_23","q_en":"Statements: All pens are books. All books are tables.\nConclusions: I. All pens are tables. II. Some tables are pens.\nWhich conclusion(s) follow?","options_en":["Only I follows","Only II follows","Both I and II follow","Neither follows"],"correct":2,"exp":"All pens are books + all books are tables ⟹ all pens are tables (I follows). Since all pens are tables, pens form a subset of tables, so at least some tables are pens (II also follows).","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Syllogism","contentType":"static","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_24","q_en":"Statements: Some doctors are teachers. All teachers are readers.\nConclusions: I. Some doctors are readers. II. All doctors are readers.\nWhich conclusion(s) follow?","options_en":["Only I follows","Only II follows","Both follow","Neither follows"],"correct":0,"exp":"Since some doctors are teachers, and all teachers are readers, those particular doctors (who are teachers) are readers — so ''some doctors are readers'' follows. But we don''t know about doctors who AREN''T teachers, so ''all doctors are readers'' does not necessarily follow.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Syllogism","contentType":"static","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_25","q_en":"Statements: No cat is a dog. All dogs are animals.\nConclusions: I. No cat is an animal. II. Some animals are dogs.\nWhich conclusion(s) follow?","options_en":["Only I follows","Only II follows","Both follow","Neither follows"],"correct":1,"exp":"''No cat is a dog'' only excludes cats from the dog category — it says nothing about animals in general, so ''no cat is an animal'' does NOT follow (cats could still be animals, just not dogs). Since all dogs are animals, dogs form a subset of animals, so ''some animals are dogs'' does follow.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Syllogism","contentType":"static","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_26","q_en":"Find the odd one out: Apple, Mango, Potato, Banana","options_en":["Potato","Mango","Apple","Banana"],"correct":0,"exp":"Apple, Mango and Banana are all fruits; Potato is a vegetable (a root vegetable, not a fruit).","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Classification (Odd One Out)","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_27","q_en":"Find the odd one out: Triangle, Square, Circle, Cube","options_en":["Circle","Triangle","Cube","Square"],"correct":2,"exp":"Triangle, Square and Circle are 2-dimensional (plane) figures; Cube is a 3-dimensional solid.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Classification (Odd One Out)","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_28","q_en":"Find the odd one out: 8, 27, 64, 100","options_en":["8","64","100","27"],"correct":2,"exp":"8 = 2³, 27 = 3³, 64 = 4³ are all perfect cubes; 100 is a perfect square (10²) but not a perfect cube.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Classification (Odd One Out)","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_29","q_en":"Find the odd one out: Delhi, Mumbai, Bengaluru, Rajasthan","options_en":["Mumbai","Bengaluru","Delhi","Rajasthan"],"correct":3,"exp":"Delhi, Mumbai and Bengaluru are cities; Rajasthan is a state, not a city.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Classification (Odd One Out)","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_30","q_en":"Doctor : Hospital :: Teacher : ?","options_en":["School","Patient","Book","Student"],"correct":0,"exp":"A Doctor works at a Hospital; by the same relation, a Teacher works at a School.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Analogy","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_31","q_en":"Pen : Write :: Knife : ?","options_en":["Cut","Sharp","Kitchen","Metal"],"correct":0,"exp":"A Pen is used to Write; by the same relation, a Knife is used to Cut.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Analogy","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_32","q_en":"Bird : Nest :: Man : ?","options_en":["House","Fly","Wing","Sky"],"correct":0,"exp":"A Bird lives in a Nest; by the same relation, a Man lives in a House.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Analogy","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_33","q_en":"5 : 25 :: 7 : ?","options_en":["49","35","14","42"],"correct":0,"exp":"The relation is squaring: 5² = 25, so following the same relation, 7² = 49.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Analogy","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_34","q_en":"Five friends A, B, C, D and E are sitting in a row facing North. B is immediate right of A. C is immediate right of B. D is immediate right of C. E is at one of the ends, to the left of A. What is the order of seating from left to right?","options_en":["E, A, B, C, D","A, B, C, D, E","E, B, A, C, D","D, C, B, A, E"],"correct":0,"exp":"B is immediately right of A → A, B adjacent in that order. C is immediately right of B → A, B, C in order. D is immediately right of C → A, B, C, D in order. E is at an end, to the left of A → E must be at the extreme left. Order: E, A, B, C, D.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Seating Arrangement","contentType":"static","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"q_csat_reason_35","q_en":"Four people P, Q, R, S are sitting around a circular table facing the centre. R is immediate right of P. Q is immediate left of P. S is between R and Q. What is the position of S with respect to P?","options_en":["Immediate right","Immediate left","Opposite","Cannot be determined"],"correct":2,"exp":"Going clockwise starting at P: P, R, S, Q (and back to P) satisfies all three clues — R immediately right of P, Q immediately left of P, and S sitting directly between R and Q. With only 4 seats, the seat between R and Q (the one not occupied by P) is directly opposite P. So S is opposite P.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Seating Arrangement","contentType":"static","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"q_csat_di_1","q_en":"Study the following table showing the number of students enrolled in a scholarship scheme across 5 districts in 2024 and 2025, then answer the questions based on it.\n\n<table><tr><th>District</th><th>2024 Enrolment</th><th>2025 Enrolment</th></tr><tr><td>Bengaluru</td><td>4200</td><td>4600</td></tr><tr><td>Mysuru</td><td>2800</td><td>3100</td></tr><tr><td>Hubballi</td><td>1900</td><td>1850</td></tr><tr><td>Kalaburagi</td><td>1500</td><td>1700</td></tr><tr><td>Belagavi</td><td>2100</td><td>2300</td></tr></table>\n\nWhat is the total enrolment across all 5 districts in 2025?","options_en":["13300","12500","13550","13650"],"correct":2,"exp":"Sum of 2025 column: 4600 + 3100 + 1850 + 1700 + 2300 = 13550.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Data Interpretation","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_di_2","q_en":"Study the following table showing the number of students enrolled in a scholarship scheme across 5 districts in 2024 and 2025, then answer the questions based on it.\n\n<table><tr><th>District</th><th>2024 Enrolment</th><th>2025 Enrolment</th></tr><tr><td>Bengaluru</td><td>4200</td><td>4600</td></tr><tr><td>Mysuru</td><td>2800</td><td>3100</td></tr><tr><td>Hubballi</td><td>1900</td><td>1850</td></tr><tr><td>Kalaburagi</td><td>1500</td><td>1700</td></tr><tr><td>Belagavi</td><td>2100</td><td>2300</td></tr></table>\n\nWhich district recorded the highest percentage growth in enrolment from 2024 to 2025?","options_en":["Bengaluru","Mysuru","Hubballi","Kalaburagi"],"correct":3,"exp":"Percentage growth = ((2025−2024)/2024)×100 for each district: Bengaluru: 9.52%, Mysuru: 10.71%, Hubballi: -2.63%, Kalaburagi: 13.33%, Belagavi: 9.52%. Kalaburagi has the highest growth at 13.33%.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Data Interpretation","contentType":"static","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"q_csat_di_3","q_en":"Study the following table showing the number of students enrolled in a scholarship scheme across 5 districts in 2024 and 2025, then answer the questions based on it.\n\n<table><tr><th>District</th><th>2024 Enrolment</th><th>2025 Enrolment</th></tr><tr><td>Bengaluru</td><td>4200</td><td>4600</td></tr><tr><td>Mysuru</td><td>2800</td><td>3100</td></tr><tr><td>Hubballi</td><td>1900</td><td>1850</td></tr><tr><td>Kalaburagi</td><td>1500</td><td>1700</td></tr><tr><td>Belagavi</td><td>2100</td><td>2300</td></tr></table>\n\nWhich district saw a DECLINE in enrolment from 2024 to 2025?","options_en":["Bengaluru","Hubballi","Kalaburagi","Mysuru"],"correct":1,"exp":"Comparing each district''s 2025 figure to 2024: only Hubballi fell, from 1900 to 1850.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Data Interpretation","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_di_4","q_en":"Study the following table showing the number of students enrolled in a scholarship scheme across 5 districts in 2024 and 2025, then answer the questions based on it.\n\n<table><tr><th>District</th><th>2024 Enrolment</th><th>2025 Enrolment</th></tr><tr><td>Bengaluru</td><td>4200</td><td>4600</td></tr><tr><td>Mysuru</td><td>2800</td><td>3100</td></tr><tr><td>Hubballi</td><td>1900</td><td>1850</td></tr><tr><td>Kalaburagi</td><td>1500</td><td>1700</td></tr><tr><td>Belagavi</td><td>2100</td><td>2300</td></tr></table>\n\nWhat is the ratio of Bengaluru''s 2025 enrolment to Mysuru''s 2025 enrolment, in simplest form?","options_en":["31:46","46:32","46:31","47:31"],"correct":2,"exp":"Bengaluru : Mysuru = 4600 : 3100. Dividing both by their HCF (100) gives 46:31.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Data Interpretation","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_di_5","q_en":"A candidate''s Mains marks are given below. Study the table and answer the questions.\n\n<table><tr><th>Paper</th><th>Max Marks</th><th>Marks Scored</th></tr><tr><td>GS1</td><td>250</td><td>178</td></tr><tr><td>GS2</td><td>250</td><td>162</td></tr><tr><td>GS3</td><td>250</td><td>190</td></tr><tr><td>GS4</td><td>250</td><td>170</td></tr><tr><td>Essay</td><td>250</td><td>155</td></tr></table>\n\nWhat is the candidate''s overall percentage across all 5 papers? (round to 2 decimals)","options_en":["66.4","68.4","70.4","0.01"],"correct":1,"exp":"Total scored = 178 + 162 + 190 + 170 + 155 = 855. Total max = 1250. Percentage = (855/1250)×100 = 68.40%.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Data Interpretation","contentType":"static","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"q_csat_di_6","q_en":"A candidate''s Mains marks are given below. Study the table and answer the questions.\n\n<table><tr><th>Paper</th><th>Max Marks</th><th>Marks Scored</th></tr><tr><td>GS1</td><td>250</td><td>178</td></tr><tr><td>GS2</td><td>250</td><td>162</td></tr><tr><td>GS3</td><td>250</td><td>190</td></tr><tr><td>GS4</td><td>250</td><td>170</td></tr><tr><td>Essay</td><td>250</td><td>155</td></tr></table>\n\nIn which paper did the candidate score the highest marks?","options_en":["GS2","GS4","GS3","GS1"],"correct":2,"exp":"Comparing scored marks: {''GS1'': 178, ''GS2'': 162, ''GS3'': 190, ''GS4'': 170, ''Essay'': 155}. The highest is GS3 with 190 marks.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Data Interpretation","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_di_7","q_en":"A candidate''s Mains marks are given below. Study the table and answer the questions.\n\n<table><tr><th>Paper</th><th>Max Marks</th><th>Marks Scored</th></tr><tr><td>GS1</td><td>250</td><td>178</td></tr><tr><td>GS2</td><td>250</td><td>162</td></tr><tr><td>GS3</td><td>250</td><td>190</td></tr><tr><td>GS4</td><td>250</td><td>170</td></tr><tr><td>Essay</td><td>250</td><td>155</td></tr></table>\n\nBy how many marks did the candidate score more in GS1 than in GS2?","options_en":["18","-16","14","16"],"correct":3,"exp":"GS1 (178) − GS2 (162) = 16 marks.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Data Interpretation","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_di_8","q_en":"What is the two-digit number?\nStatement I: The sum of its digits is 9.\nStatement II: The number is divisible by 9 and lies between 40 and 50.","options_en":["Statement I alone is sufficient","Statement II alone is sufficient","Both together are needed","Neither is sufficient"],"correct":1,"exp":"Statement I alone (digit sum = 9) fits many numbers (18, 27, 36, 45, 54...) — not sufficient alone. Statement II alone: two-digit multiples of 9 between 40 and 50 — checking 41 to 49, only 45 (9×5) qualifies — so Statement II alone is sufficient.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Data Sufficiency","contentType":"static","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"q_csat_di_9","q_en":"What is the LCM of 12 and 18?","options_en":["72","36","29","43"],"correct":1,"exp":"12 = 2²×3, 18 = 2×3². LCM takes the highest power of each prime: 2²×3² = 36.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Basic Numeracy","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_di_10","q_en":"What is the HCF of 24 and 36?","options_en":["15","9","12","24"],"correct":2,"exp":"24 = 2³×3, 36 = 2²×3². HCF takes the lowest power of each common prime: 2²×3 = 12.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Basic Numeracy","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_di_11","q_en":"What is the value of √144?","options_en":["15","24","9","12"],"correct":3,"exp":"12 × 12 = 144, so √144 = 12.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Basic Numeracy","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"q_csat_di_12","q_en":"If 3 pens cost ₹45, what is the cost of 7 pens (at the same rate)?","options_en":["52","105","123","87"],"correct":1,"exp":"Cost per pen = 45/3 = ₹15. Cost of 7 pens = 15×7 = ₹105.","subject":"CSAT","subTopic":"Basic Numeracy","contentType":"static","difficulty":"easy"}]'::jsonb,
  0, false, 'CSAT'
);